<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Lara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Lara was one of the most powerful noble families of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, named after Lara de los Infantes near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>. In the 12th century its members, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manrique-perez-de-lara">Manrique Pérez de Lara</a>, dominated the regency of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> in rivalry with the Castro family and held the Lordship of Molina. The family remained prominent in Castilian politics into the 14th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-lara.jpg" alt="House of Lara" width="320" height="663" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HeralderElements from SanchoPanzaXXIFile:House of Lara COA.svg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_House_of_Lara.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family arose in 11th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> through a marriage that joined the lands of Gonzalo Núñez around Lara de los Infantes with the inheritance of his wife, Goto Núñez, who represented the holdings of the noble Álvarez and Alfonso families. By the 13th century the Lara family was linked to the heroes of the Cantar de los Siete Infantes de Lara, a 10th-century tale of revenge set in their lands, but there is no evidence that the tale reflects real events, and its names cannot be matched with the family's known ancestry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 12th to the 15th century members of the family by turns supported and opposed their kings. In 1113 Pedro González de Lara backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urraca-of-leon-and-castile">Queen Urraca</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> against her former husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a>, and in 1130 he and his brother Rodrigo confronted her son Alfonso VII, though Rodrigo later helped Alfonso against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>. Manrique Pérez de Lara, Álvar Pérez and Nuño Pérez de Lara disputed the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a>, whose alférez was Fernando Núñez de Lara, and Álvaro Núñez de Lara became regent for Henry I of Castile.</p><p>Nuño González de Lara served Ferdinand III and Alfonso X but in 1270 led an alliance of nobles against Alfonso. Juan Núñez I de Lara, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a>, opposed the accession of Sancho IV and had to flee for a time to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Juan Núñez II de Lara led several rebellions against Alfonso XI. Peter the Cruel stripped the family of much of its land, most of which was returned by his half-brother and rival Henry II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The only branch to survive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> was the Manrique de Lara, who supported the Catholic Monarchs against the partisans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-la-beltraneja">Joanna la Beltraneja</a>. In 1520 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> made this line Grandees of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najera">Nájera</a> and Marquises of Aguilar de Campoo, and its members served the crown as viceroys, captains general, ambassadors and cardinals. The writers Gómez Manrique and Jorge Manrique belonged to it, and members of the family established branches as far away as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Lara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Counts and great nobles, such as the rival <b>Lara</b> and Castro families, held lands and offices from the king, and their quarrels dominated royal minorities, above all that of Alfonso VIII after 1158.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In about 1129 Alfonso I of Aragon took the town, and it was soon granted to the <b>Lara</b> family, becoming the Lordship of Molina.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-molina">Taifa of Molina</a></li></ul></section>
